  "notes": "Dru Hill's frontman solo, on Tim & Bob's string-driven crossover pop-R&B, 1999–2001. The research calls this \"the widest split on the roster: ballads 60–61 half-time, 'Thong Song' 130 straight\", and the crossover half is what is encoded — 102–130 straight with the mode at 130 — because every sourced parameter in the entry (both keys, the modulation, the string source, the personnel) belongs to that record; his 60–61 ballad band is the Dru Hill grammar, already modelled under that name. Three drum deltas: a fast straight four with the kick on 1 and 3 and `fourOnFloorInChorus` true, a clap layer stacked flat on 2 and 4 with `offGridMs` 0 and `swing.amount` 0.50, and a continuous 16th hat at 0.6 fill density with the open hat on both offbeats — nothing in this lane runs this fast, so the whole kit is a delta. Harmony is \"minor vamps with a semitone final modulation\", the sourced key pair being C♯ minor moving to D minor; the modulation itself has no schema field and is recorded here. The identifying parameter is the counter: \"an **obbligato string line** (Bruce Dukov) running through the whole record as the hook\", so `countermelody.densityRatio` is 0.7, the highest in this section, at +12 with `answer_lick` and `arp` sharing weight. The bass is \"driving synth bass in 8ths on the uptempo (Latin-pop-derived)\", encoded as `offbeat_8ths` with repeated-root cells — the only `offbeat_8ths` model in the section. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all velocity and density numbers and the melody register. ⚠ `session.keys` is part-sourced: the entry names `C#m`, `Dm`, and the remaining values `Bm`, `Am`, together with all 4 weights, are INFERRED rather than read from the entry.",
